  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of balloons that pop.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=15$ balloons\n\n- the probability that one balloon pops is $p=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\u223cB(15,\\frac{1}{5}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{x}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})}^{15-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{x}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting at least $3$ balloons that pop. This corresponds to $P(X>2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+\u2026+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{0}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{0}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{1}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{1}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{2}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-2}] \\\\  & =1-[{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15}+15(\\frac{1}{5}){(\\frac{4}{5})}^{14}+105{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{13}] \\\\  & =1-0.398\\,023 \\\\  & =0.602\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
